Thus, for arbitrary $\ket{\phi}$ and $\ket{\psi}$, $\braket{\phi(t)}{\psi(t)}
=
\braket{\phi(0)}{\psi(0)}.$ Substituting the evolution laws gives $\bra{\phi(0)}U^\dagger(t)U(t)\ket{\psi(0)}
=
\braket{\phi(0)}{\psi(0)}.$ Since this equation holds for every pair of vectors, $\boxed{U^\dagger(t)U(t)=I}.$ Such an operator is unitary. The scalar analogue is a complex number of unit magnitude, $e^{i\theta}\bigl(e^{i\theta}\bigr)^*=1.$ A unitary operator is the matrix generalization of a pure phase: it can rotate a state through Hilbert space, but it cannot stretch or shrink it.

