p.src:
Use $\ket{\psi(t+\epsilon)}
=
\left(I-\frac{i\epsilon}{\hbar}H\right)\ket{\psi(t)}.$ Then $\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dt}\ket{\psi(t)}
&=
\lim_{\epsilon\to0}
\frac{\left(I-\frac{i\epsilon}{\hbar}H\right)\ket{\psi(t)}
-\ket{\psi(t)}}{\epsilon} \\
&=
-\frac{i}{\hbar}H\ket{\psi(t)}.\end{aligned}$ Equivalently, $\boxed{
i\hbar\frac{d}{dt}\ket{\psi(t)}
=H\ket{\psi(t)}
}.$ This is the Schrödinger equation. Once $H$ and the state at one instant are known, it determines the state at later times. Probabilities for later measurements then follow from the ordinary measurement rules.

