We have followed the state while keeping observables fixed. This is the Schrödinger picture. Let $A$ be an observable with no explicit time dependence. Its expectation value is $\langle A\rangle_t
=
\bra{\psi(t)}A\ket{\psi(t)}.$ Differentiate both the bra and the ket: $\frac{d}{dt}\langle A\rangle_t
=
\bra{\dot\psi}A\ket{\psi}
+
\bra{\psi}A\ket{\dot\psi}.$ The Schrödinger equation and its Hermitian conjugate give $\ket{\dot\psi}
=-\frac{i}{\hbar}H\ket{\psi},
\qquad
\bra{\dot\psi}
=\frac{i}{\hbar}\bra{\psi}H.$ Hence $\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dt}\langle A\rangle_t
&=
\frac{i}{\hbar}\bra{\psi}HA\ket{\psi}
-\frac{i}{\hbar}\bra{\psi}AH\ket{\psi} \\
&=
\frac{i}{\hbar}\bra{\psi}(HA-AH)\ket{\psi}.\end{aligned}$ Define the commutator $=HA-AH.$ Then the equation of motion for an expectation value is $\boxed{
\frac{d}{dt}\langle A\rangle_t
=
\frac{i}{\hbar}\langle[H,A]\rangle_t
}.$

