p.src:
At $t=0$, the coefficient of $\ket{\downarrow\uparrow}$ vanishes, as it must. At later times the relative phase between the singlet and triplet components makes this coefficient grow. Define the mean energy and splitting, $\overline E=\frac{E_T+E_S}{2},
\qquad
\Delta E=E_T-E_S.$ Factoring out the mean-energy phase gives $\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\ket{\psi(t)}
&=e^{-i\overline E t/\hbar}\Biggl[
\cos\!\left(\frac{\Delta E\,t}{2\hbar}\right)
\ket{\uparrow\downarrow}
\\
&\qquad
-i\sin\!\left(\frac{\Delta E\,t}{2\hbar}\right)
\ket{\downarrow\uparrow}
\Biggr]
\end{aligned}
}.$

